ng point in Georgia's post-Soviet trajectory, influencing both domestic politics and international relations in the region. Its establishment marked a shift in the political landscape, challenging the dominance of the United National Movement and aiming to forge a new identity for Georgia in the wake of its independence from the Soviet Union.

